    "result_text": "I've implemented the Cp51 \"level-ceiling\" specification. Here's what I did and an important honest caveat.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe spec: *a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum `membershipLevel` among their household members; with no existing household member, no cap applies.*\n\n1. **Stored the level** \u2014 added a `membership_level` column (all four DB schemas) and a `membershipLevel` field/accessors on `Owner`, since the cap depends on *other* owners and can't be derived from a single owner in the mapper.\n2. **Cap computation** \u2014 in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`, after household size is known, `cappedMembershipLevel(owner)` computes the natural level (`Memberships.membershipLevel`) then caps it to `min(natural, maxHouseholdMemberLevel + 1)`; with no existing member it returns the natural level uncapped. A helper `effectiveMembershipLevel` reads each existing member's stored level (falling back to computed for seed data).\n3. **Mapper** \u2014 `OwnerMapper.membershipLevel` now returns the stored value when present, else falls back to the computed value (so seed/legacy owners still work).\n4. **Allowed household joining** \u2014 Cp51's test creates a *second* owner in the same household without `sharesHousehold` and expects success (201). The pre-existing outright household-duplicate rejection returned 409 for exactly that case, so I removed it (exact-identity duplicates are still rejected by `rejectDuplicateIdentity`). This matches the spec's premise that a new owner *can* have household members.\n\n**`Cp51Tests` passes, and the project builds successfully.**\n\n## Important caveat \u2014 the suite cannot be fully green\n\nThe 51 `CpNN` acceptance tests are the *entire* suite, and they are **independent, mutually-contradictory checkpoints** of the same endpoint, not a cumulative spec. Cp51 directly contradicts four earlier ones, which now fail:\n\n| Test | Asserts | Conflict with Cp51 |\n|------|---------|--------------------|\n| Cp10, Cp35 | same lastName+postcode \u2192 **409** | Cp51 requires it **created (201)** |\n| Cp23, Cp39 | household's 3rd member \u2192 **uncapped level 3** | Cp51's cap forbids exceeding max+1 |\n\nThere is no implementation that satisfies Cp51's specification *and* these four simultaneously \u2014 the requirements are logical opposites (identical request preconditions, opposite expected outcomes). Baseline had 1 failure (unimplemented Cp51); it now has 4, all being the checkpoints Cp51 supersedes. Every other test still passes, and I verified no unrelated regressions.\n\nIf the intent was instead to leave household rejection intact and only add the cap, Cp51 cannot pass (its second create 409s) \u2014 so I prioritized the explicit requirement that the new test go green while implementing the spec faithfully.",
